Subject: [PATCH] [REQ-180] Restore REQ-164's absent-target preservation case
 (Route A)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Implements: do-work/working/REQ-180-restore-http-archive-staging-and-validation.md

Of a 284-line commit, one test branch was the correct restoration.

Superseded: a73c3e4 did not just re-implement REQ-164's staging, it replaced the
file with an os.Root-confined design that is strictly stronger — private randomized
O_EXCL staging, non-regular target refusal, and identity-plus-digest re-checks
immediately before publication, using Link for an absent target and Rename otherwise.
Re-applying createArchiveStage/publishArchive would have been a downgrade.

Mostly superseded: TestFetchArchiveRefusesUnsafeTargetsUnchanged already covers what
REQ-164's two per-route refusal tests covered, and proves both routes report "not
attempted" — stronger than testing them separately.

Clobbered: REQ-164 had RENAMED the preservation test while broadening it from one
case to two. a73c3e4 restored the narrow pre-REQ-164 body under the old name, so the
absent-target case — a failed fetch must not CREATE a target that was never there —
was silently gone.

- Parameterize TestTotalFailurePreservesTheTargetAndLeavesNoScratch over pre-existing
  and absent targets
- Leave archive_fetch.go byte-identical to HEAD

Proved by mutation. Disabling validation was not enough: it makes the fetch succeed,
exercising a different failure. Removing private staging and stage cleanup — the bug
the assertion actually guards — fails the two cases on different lines, the absent one
on "a failed fetch created the absent target". Mutations reverted.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
